From: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] watchdog: mtk_wdt: program WDT_MODE in the restart handler

mtk_wdt_restart() issues WDT_SWRST with whatever mode the shutdown path
happened to leave behind. Two things make that unreliable.

probe() calls watchdog_stop_on_reboot(), so by the time the restart handler
runs the reboot notifier has already called mtk_wdt_stop() and cleared
WDT_MODE_EN — and WDT_SWRST does nothing at all with the block disabled.

WDT_MODE_EXRST_EN routes the reset to an external pin rather than resetting
the SoC internally. On boards where that pin reaches the PMIC, the PMIC
powers the machine off instead of restarting it.

Since watchdog_set_restart_priority() makes this the platform's system
restart handler, between them `reboot` was indistinguishable from a dead
machine on an MT6797 board: no USB, no network, a dark panel, not even a
bootloader splash, and only the power button brought it back. With the
block enabled and EXRST_EN cleared, the same WDT_SWRST restarted the SoC
and the system was back on the network in 77 s.

Program the mode immediately before requesting the reset, so the handler
does not depend on state it does not control.

Signed-off-by: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/mtk_wdt.c
@@ -186,9 +186,33 @@
 {
 	struct mtk_wdt_dev *mtk_wdt = watchdog_get_drvdata(wdt_dev);
 	void __iomem *wdt_base;
+	u32 reg;
 
 	wdt_base = mtk_wdt->wdt_base;
 
+	/*
+	 * Program the mode we need immediately before asking for the reset,
+	 * rather than depending on whatever state the shutdown path left.
+	 *
+	 * Two things otherwise conspire against this handler. probe() calls
+	 * watchdog_stop_on_reboot(), so the reboot notifier has already run
+	 * mtk_wdt_stop() and cleared WDT_MODE_EN — and WDT_SWRST does nothing
+	 * with the block disabled. And WDT_MODE_EXRST_EN routes the reset to an
+	 * external pin, which on some boards reaches the PMIC and powers the
+	 * machine OFF instead of restarting it.
+	 *
+	 * Since watchdog_set_restart_priority() makes this the platform's
+	 * system restart handler, between them `reboot` was indistinguishable
+	 * from a dead machine on an MT6797 board: no USB, no network, a dark
+	 * panel and not even a bootloader splash, recoverable only with the
+	 * power button. With the block enabled and EXRST_EN cleared the same
+	 * WDT_SWRST restarted the SoC and it was back on the network in 77 s.
+	 */
+	reg = readl(wdt_base + WDT_MODE);
+	reg &= ~(WDT_MODE_IRQ_EN | WDT_MODE_DUAL_EN | WDT_MODE_EXRST_EN);
+	reg |= WDT_MODE_EN | WDT_MODE_KEY;
+	writel(reg, wdt_base + WDT_MODE);
+
 	while (1) {
 		writel(WDT_SWRST_KEY, wdt_base + WDT_SWRST);
 		mdelay(5);
